  "title": "Chapter 24: High Court in India",
  "order": 25,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zpzmge6",
      "content": "The High Court is the highest judicial authority at the state level in India. It functions under Part VI, Chapter V of the Constitution of India. The provisions regarding High Courts are mainly contained in Articles 214–231."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Constitutional Part",
          "value": "Part VI",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Articles Range",
          "value": "Art. 214 - 231",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Retirement Age",
          "value": "62 Years",
          "icon": "clock"
        },
        {
          "label": "Writ Article",
          "value": "Article 226",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "i4ijkzc"
    },
    {
      "id": "czq1jia",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Constitutional Articles Related to High Courts"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Article 214 provides that there shall be a High Court for each state, though Parliament may establish a common High Court for two or more states.",
        "High Court judges are appointed by the President of India after consultation with the Chief Justice of India, the Governor, and the Chief Justice of the concerned High Court.",
        "A judge of a High Court holds office until the age of 62 years and submits resignation directly to the President.",
        "High Courts exercise jurisdiction over writs under Article 226 for enforcement of fundamental rights and other legal rights."
      ],
      "id": "dp00xll"
    },
